WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.199
Good morning.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.119
My name is Michael Hall.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:22.759
I'm a professor of Biochemistry at the Biozentrum of the University of Basel.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.639
And I work on... on cell growth and iBiology has asked me to tell you the story of the

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.519
discovery of... of TOR.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:39.039
Now, TOR is a is a kinase, it's a highly conserved kinase, and more importantly it's a central

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.399
controller of cell growth, and this is the story I'll tell you today.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.199
So, this is the timeline of the TOR field.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.799
The story actually starts in 1965 and that's when a group of scientists from Montreal set

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:57.679
out for Easter Island, known as Rapa Nui to the locals, and they were prospecting for...

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.719
for exotic microbes which produced natural products or secondary metabolites, which they

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.679
could then develop into a... an antifungal.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.399
They did isolate a bacterium from the soil; it was a Streptomyces from which they isolated

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.199
a compound which they called rapamycin, after Rapa Nui.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.519
However, when they started to develop this compound as an antifungal, they realized it

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.359
had the undesirable side effect of suppressing the immune system.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.679
So, it was then dropped and was never further developed as an antifungal.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:39.079
Years later, when immunosuppressive therapy came into existence, this drug was then rediscovered,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.719
but now for use as an immunosuppressive.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.919
So, it was now developed for the very reason for which it had originally been rejected.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.879
So, we started our studies on... on rapamycin, trying to lose state the molecular mechanism

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.319
of rapamycin action.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:06.559
And this story was started by... by an outstanding postdoc who joined the lab in 1989, Joe Heitman.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:13.479
He was a MD/PhD who just finished his PhD studies at... in New York at Rockefeller University

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:20.239
and, given his medical background, was interested in understanding how... how drugs functioned.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.479
And we also... another fortuitous circumstance was an... an ongoing collaboration with Rao

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:31.959
Movva, who was a group leader at a local Basel company by the name of Sandoz, which is now

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.879
known as Novartis.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:41.839
Now, Joe and Rao had the brilliant idea to study the mechanism of action of rapamycin

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.679
by taking an approach, a genetic approach, with the simple eukaryote yeast --

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.159
Saccharomyces cerevisiae, in particular.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.759
Now, this was a brilliant idea because not only did it allow one to use genetics to go

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:05.679
after the target of rapamycin, but it was also an unusual idea, because, at that time,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.199
rapamycin was being developed for a... as a drug for use on humans, and everybody else

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.439
was of course working on mammalian cells to understand how this drug worked.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.719
But Joe and Rao remembered that rapamycin had originally been isolated as an antifungal,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.359
and you could therefore use...

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.959
use yeast, and yeast genetics in particular, to... to study rapamycin action.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:38.919
So, what Joe did was he grew up a culture of our... of our standard lab yeast strain

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.439
and then plated it on... on solid media containing rapamycin.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:49.159
And the rapamycin killed most of the cells which... which were plated on this... on this

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.519
petri dish, but a few spontaneous mutants arose.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.199
He then picked these mutants, these and several others.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.399
He characterized about a total of 20 different rapamycin-resistant mutants, at least in the

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.319
early phases of the study, and he then purified each one of these individually... mutants,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.319
and then characterized them.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:16.439
More particularly, he... he determined the number of complementation groups and what

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.039
genes were mutated in these... in these... in these strains to...

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.839
to confer rapamycin resistance.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.959
So, Joe found that these uhh... rapamycin-resistant yeast mutants were defective in any one of

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.319
three different genes.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.119
The vast majority of the mutations were recessive and in a gene called fpr1.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.039
This is actually a gene which Joe had already characterized in the lab in the context of

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.159
his earlier studies on a... on a rapamycin-like drug called FK506.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:54.559
He also obtained mutants in two novel genes -- these mutations were extremely rare and,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.959
unlike the fpr1 mutations, were dominant.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:07.119
So, this was the first... the first appearance in the literature, in 1991, of... of TOR,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.319
so this was the original identification of TOR, in this case as

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.039
genetic loci in the yeast genome.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:24.359
But we still didn't know what the TOR genes encoded, nor did we know why these mutations

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.399
were dominant and... and rare.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:34.359
Now, to answer these questions, two new students joined the project.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:41.519
One was a Jeanette Kunz, an extremely bright and hardworking Swiss student,

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.559
and then Stephen Helli... Helliwell,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:49.159
a British student who was one of the more colorful members of our... of our

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.879
lab, as you can probably guess from this photograph.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:06:00.599
So, what Jeanette and Stephen did was they cloned the... the TOR genes from our rapamycin-resistant

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:11.559
yeast mutants and sequenced these... these... these genes, both the wild-type version and

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.679
the rapamycin-resistance-conferring version.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:22.679
And this allowed us to understand why the TOR mutants were... were dominant and rare,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:29.599
and the fpr1 mutants were... were recessive and... and common.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.119
And the uhh.. and the reason relates the mechanism of action of rapamycin -- and that's shown

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.279
on this slide.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.679
This is rapamycin, drawn in red, here.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.639
This is a very lipophilic molecule, so it simply diffuses across the plasma membrane.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.039
It does not require any kind of transporter, which when mutated

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.359
could confer rapamycin resistance.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.519
And, once inside the cell, it then forms a complex with another very small, 12 kiloDalton

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.039
protein called FKBP, which is absolutely essential for drug action.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:08.319
The... the toxic agent is the rapamycin/FKBP complex -- the drug alone does nothing.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:15.679
And the fpr1, or the FKBP, protein is not only completely essential for drug action

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.119
but it's not required for cell viability.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:23.839
So, any simple loss-of-function mutation in the gene encoding this FKBP protein confers

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:30.479
complete rapamycin resistance, and such mutations would be recessive.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:37.199
That's why most mutations were in the FKBP-encoding gene and why these mutations were recessive.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.039
TOR, on the other hand, and this is just a very small portion of TOR.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:47.159
TOR is about a 300 kiloDalton protein, as compared to this 12 kiloDalton protein, here,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:52.319
so you can see this is indeed a very small part of the... of the TOR protein.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.839
This is the FKBP/rapamycin binding site in... in TOR.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:07.399
And, unlike FKBP, TOR is completely essential for cell viability.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:11.239
And therefore it can tolerate very little mutation.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:18.439
In fact, every mutation we obtained which fell into the TOR gene modified a... one residue

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:25.239
-- they all fell in the same codon -- which modified a residue in this alpha helix, which

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.439
was a key contact site between TOR and rapamycin.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:35.959
So, the effect of these mutations was to prevent the binding of rapamycin to... to TOR, without

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.679
otherwise affecting TOR activity.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:45.439
And, given that these mutations were confined to this single codon, this is why the... these

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.999
mutations were extremely rare, and of course they conferred rapamycin resistance in the

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.679
presence of a... of a wild-type copy of the TOR gene, which is why they were dominant.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.359
So, these mutations were extremely informative.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.679
They not only helped elucidate the mechanism action of rapamycin, it forms... the fact

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:10.759
it forms a complex of FKBP, it also led to the identification of the binding sites in...

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:16.679
in the TOR protein, and, most importantly, led to the identification of TOR itself.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:22.839
So, once we cloned the... the TOR genes and sequenced them, we found, first of all, that

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:32.199
the... the two TOR genes encode very similar proteins -- these proteins are 70% identical.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:39.599
And they also turned out to be the founding members of this class of kinases, protein

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.239
kinases, called PI kinase-related protein kinases.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:49.399
And the reason for that is they... they... they... all the members of this class of atypical

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:55.479
protein kinases resembled lipid kinases, PI kinases in particular.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:02.479
Since its early discovery of TOR in yeast, TOR has been found, now, in all eukaryotes,

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.759
from yeast all the way to human.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.959
And the name TOR -- from yeast -- has been kept for all these organisms.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:16.199
In the case of human TOR, or mammalian TOR, call it mTOR for mammalian TOR.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.439
So, this is where we were in 1993 after the cloning and sequencing of our...

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.599
of our TOR mutants.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:33.079
We knew that TOR existed and it was the copy of the... the target of a FKBP/rapamycin complex.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:38.279
And, in this model, you can actually see... this is the time where we still thought TOR

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:45.199
was a lipid kinase, a PI kinase in particular, as indicated by the PI, the phosphoinositol,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.319
in this figure.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.359
We had no idea at the time what was upstream and what was actually downstream of TOR.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.039
We also incorrectly thought the role of TOR was to control G1 progression, in other words

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:57.759
to control cell division.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.039
But this turned out to be wrong.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:07.439
And, in fact, the role of TOR is to control cell growth.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.799
And here I have to make an important distinction between cell growth, which is an increase

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:18.839
in cell size or cell mass, versus cell division, which is an increase in cell number.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:24.079
We never expected that the TOR would be a controller of cell growth because, as hard

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.839
as it might be to believe from the perspective of today, in those days nobody thought cell

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.119
growth was actively controlled.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:37.119
In other words, there would be no system to control cell growth; it was thought to be

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:44.159
a spontaneous process that just happens when nutrients are... or building blocks are available.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:53.759
So, we had to do additional research to eventually arrive at the conclusion that the role of

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.079
TOR2 is to control cell growth.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.119
And we came to this... to this conclusion based on a great deal of work -- not only

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:06.599
from our lab, but a large number of other largely yeast labs in this in those times

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:11.079
-- which showed that TOR controls a large number of cellular processes.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:18.479
Now, these cellular processes can be subdivided into two groups: the anabolic processes which

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:24.959
TOR activates, and the catabolic processes which TOR inhibits.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:32.999
So, TOR balances these opposing forces of synthesis and degradation in response to,

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.439
and this is important... in response to nutrients.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:40.239
And this is also something we discovered in the mid-1990s, that the...

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.599
TOR is activated by... by nutrients.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:54.799
So... and then this led to the model that TOR is a central controller of cell growth.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:13:03.039
This is most photogenically illustrated in experiments done in Drosophila by Tom Neufeld

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:06.319
and then Bruce Edgar.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:10.279
On the far side, here, we have an experiment done by Tom Neufeld.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:17.039
This is the fat body of a fruit fly and what Tom did was he selectively inactivated TOR

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.119
in these two GFP-expressing cells.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:26.679
And, as you can see, these cells are smaller than their neighboring wild-type cells.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.159
Bruce Edgar did the inverse experiment.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:35.559
What he did was he hyperactivated TOR signaling in these GFP-expressing cells, and he observed

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.319
the opposite effect -- the cells became much larger.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:43.639
So, the obvious conclusion from these experiments, which were largely confirmation of work done

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:50.199
earlier in yeast, was that the role of TOR is to... is to control cell growth and thereby

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.639
cell size.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:57.719
Now, Tom Neufeld took this experiment one step further by, now, isolating a mutant fly,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:03.079
a TOR mutant fly in which all cells of the entire animal are defective for TOR signaling.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:07.919
And it's important to point out that this TOR mutant flies a so-called hypomorph, so

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:09.479
it's a partial loss-of-function.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:15.319
If it had been a... a complete loss-of-function, this fly never would have been born given

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:18.199
that TOR is absolutely essential for viability.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:23.359
So, when Tom Neufeld isolated this fly, he then asked the question, why is this fly smaller?

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.359
Is it smaller because it has fewer cells?

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:30.039
Or is it smaller because it has the normal number of cells but now each individual cell

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:31.119
is slightly smaller?

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:36.359
And he could answer this question by... by looking in the wing, where he could... at

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:42.559
the wing is a plane of cells, so he could relatively easily measure the sizes of individual

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:47.919
cells and count the number of cells, and from that he could extrapolate to the whole animal.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:52.079
And what he found was this TOR mutant fly had more or less the normal number of cells,

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.119
but each individual cell was slightly smaller.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:00.399
So, the conclusion from this is... is that the role of TOR is not only to control cell

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:07.079
size, but thereby also to control size of individual tissues and ultimately size of

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.319
the whole animal.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:14.879
So, TOR controls growth very broadly -- broadly in terms of organism and broadly in terms

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:20.159
of physiological context, essentially any... any situation in eukaryotic biology where

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:26.519
you see a change in cell size, there's a high likelihood that TOR will be somehow involved.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.599
So, here we had another conundrum.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:36.439
We had known earlier from our... from our genetic studies in yeast that there are two

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:43.319
TOR genes, two TOR proteins in yeast, and whereas these two proteins were similar, 70%

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.399
identical, they were not functionally identical.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:51.799
And this came from the observation that if we knocked out the TOR1 gene nothing happened,

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:58.239
so we proposed that... that the TOR2 then has some redundant function with... with TOR1.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:02.239
If we knocked out TOR2 alone the cells died,

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.479
but they didn't die in the G1 phase of the cell cycle.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:11.839
And, finally, if we knocked out both TOR genes simultaneously, the cells of course still

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.679
died, but now they died in the G1 phase of the cell cycle.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:21.999
This told us that TOR2 has two functions, TOR1 has one function, and that one function

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:27.119
of TOR1 overlaps with one of the two functions of TOR2.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:34.679
So, we didn't know what these two so-called functions were and how they

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.359
controlled cell growth.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:47.159
Well, again, a great deal of work from our and a number of other labs doing yeast genetics

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:54.519
found that these two separate functions of TOR are in fact two separate signaling pathways.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:17:03.359
One is the... what we call the TOR2-unique pathway, and this signals to the actin cytoskeleton.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:12.439
And... and we therefore view this as a pathway which mediates spatial control of cell growth.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:22.279
The other function of TOR, the one that either TOR1 or TOR2 can perform, this controls all

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:29.199
these processes which lead to mass accumulation in response to nutrients, and we view this...

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:34.039
this signaling pathway... this signaling branch of TOR as mediating

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.479
temporal control of cell growth.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:43.079
So, we think the logic of these two different TOR signaling pathways is that they integrate

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:45.799
spatial and temporal control of cell growth.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:50.479
Of course, these two aspects of cell growth need to be integrated.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:56.599
So, at this stage, we had another problem and that is, what determines the specificity

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.519
of the... of the TORs?

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:05.119
Why can TOR2 signal through both pathways but TOR1 only through one?

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:12.119
And we also didn't know why the TOR-shared branch was rapamycin sensitive, whereas the

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.359
TOR2-unique branch was rapamycin resistant.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:22.199
And to tackle these problems, another postdoc joined the lab, Canadian postdoc Robbie Loewith,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:28.119
who teamed up with my long-term technician, Wolfgang Oppliger, and they decided... unlike

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:32.839
our previous work, decided to take a biochemical approach to understanding TOR signaling.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:44.519
So, Robbie did this, he purified the two TOR proteins, and what he found was that the TORs

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:49.639
did in fact purify... co-purify with other proteins, and we had these purified or what

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:53.119
we now call TOR complexes...

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:58.879
TOR complex 1 and TOR complex 2... when he had these two purified TORCs in the test tube,

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:01.319
we sequenced the proteins.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:07.199
This all... allowed us isolate the genes encoding all these subunits of the two TOR complexes

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:15.359
and, once we had the... the genes isolated, he was able to knock these genes out.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:20.799
And when he did this, he found that these two TOR complexes corresponded to these two

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:24.759
previously identified TOR signaling pathways.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:32.359
So, at this stage, we started wondering whether the TOR complexes, like TOR itself, might

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:35.839
be conserved all the way to human.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:40.439
But, to address this question, another postdoc joined the lab, Estela Jacinto.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:45.999
She joined the lab from Michael Karin's lab and was the first person in my lab to start

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:53.119
studying TOR in mammalian cells to ask this specific question, is... is TOR or are the

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.919
TOR complexes conserved in... in mammals?

00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:05.639
So, she and... and notably other members of the TOR field at this time, most importantly

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:12.639
David Sabatini, Kazu Yonezawa, and Kun-Liang Guan, showed that the two complexes indeed

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.959
are conserved in humans.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:20.599
They are made up of the same proteins, or orthologues of the proteins, that were originally

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.479
discovered in yeast.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:28.839
They phosphorylate or act on many of the same downstream proteins to control the same cellular

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:32.199
processes involved in cell growth.

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:38.359
So, the picture that emerges of the TOR signaling network... and we call it a network because

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:45.679
it's more than a single pathway... the TOR signaling network is a primordial or ancestral

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:51.599
signaling network which has been conserved all the way from yeast to human to control

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:55.119
this very fundamental process of cell growth.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:21:01.279
Now, the sole exception to that statement I just made is this part of the network up

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:05.999
here, which is the growth factor or insulin signaling pathway.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:07.679
This evolved much later.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:13.759
This evolved with multicellularity and was then grafted on to the more primordial TOR

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:18.599
signaling pathway which already existed in unicellular yeast.

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:26.999
And the reason for this is that cell growth control in metazoans is more complex than

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.799
unicellular organisms, because in metazoans it's critical to control the growth of every

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:37.599
cell in the body with every other cell in the body, so you need an input which controls

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:39.719
growth over a whole body plan.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:44.839
And this is then what the growth factor signaling input provides, this control of cell growth

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.079
over a whole body plan.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:51.679
Now, I'd like to end with a note on the clinical relevance of... of... of TOR.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:58.639
We now know, based on work over the last 15 years, that TOR signaling is... is functionally

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:05.599
linked to a large number of diseases, all of which are characterized by inappropriate

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.879
or ectopic cell growth.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:12.879
These can be malignant forms of cell growth such as cancer or more benign forms of cell

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:20.359
growth like cardiac hypertrophy, but not... but in all cases cell growth, which also underscores

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:25.079
the... the... the function of TOR as a controller of cell growth.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:31.999
More recently, TOR has been implicated in another set of disorders, the so-called metabolic

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:37.799
disorders, and we know that high... chronically high circulating levels of nutrients can upregulate

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:41.159
TOR even in the absence of growth factors.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:48.999
This will lead to adipogenesis and obesity, which of course can lead to type 2 diabetes.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:54.199
But we have a more direct link to type 2 diabetes in the TOR signaling network, and that is

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:58.919
that TOR complex 1 can negatively feedback on the insulin signaling pathway

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:02.479
by phosphorylating IRS.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:09.839
As a result of this, high levels of TOR can lead to insulin insensitivity, which is one

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.999
of the hallmarks of type 2 diabetes.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:19.319
In this context, some have actually proposed using rapamycin as an anti-diabetic drug,

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:23.719
so this would be a fourth major therapeutic area for this remarkable drug.

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:28.919
However, I don't think anybody's actually seriously developing rapamycin as an anti-diabetic

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:33.239
because there are a number of other complications.

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:41.159
But at least one finding, or one... one thing that gives credence to this notion that rapamycin

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:45.479
could be developed into an anti-diabetic is that the world's most commonly prescribed

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.799
anti-diabetic, metformin, works at least in part

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:52.479
by down-regulating the TOR signaling pathway.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:24:00.919
So, I've come to the end of the story of the discovery of TOR, or of these three sub-discoveries

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:02.599
of TOR signaling.

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:09.239
The story I told you spanned the years of 1990 to 2004.

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:12.599
A lot has happened since 2004.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.439
The size of the field has grown even further.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:20.159
There's still a great deal of exciting biology, but that will have to be a story

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:38.960
for another day.

